---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} is built and why everything runs in your browser.`}
>
  <div class="tool-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>{SITE.tagline}</p>
  </div>

  <section class="card prose">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A small, growing set of tools for the jobs that come up mid-task — generate a password, build
      a passphrase, hash a file — without opening a service that wants an account first.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      The whole site is static HTML, CSS and JavaScript, built with Astro and served from a CDN.
      There is no backend and no database, so there is nothing to breach and nothing to leak. Every
      tool does its work in your browser. A few pieces load on demand the first time you need them —
      a wordlist, the YAML parser — so a page you have already used keeps working offline.
    </p>

    <h2>Randomness</h2>
    <p>
      Anything random comes from <code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>, the browser&rsquo;s
      cryptographically secure generator, with rejection sampling so values are drawn uniformly.
      <code>Math.random()</code> is never used: its state is recoverable from a few outputs, which
      would make anything derived from it predictable.
    </p>

    <h2>The one dependency</h2>
    <p>
      Everything here is written from scratch except YAML parsing, which uses
      <a href="https://github.com/nodeca/js-yaml">js-yaml</a> (MIT). YAML is a large specification
      with genuinely surprising corners, and a formatter that quietly misreads your document is
      worse than no formatter at all. It is bundled with the site rather than loaded from a CDN, and
      only the YAML page ever downloads it.
    </p>
    <p>
      MD5 is the other thing worth naming: <code>crypto.subtle</code> refuses to implement it, so
      the hash tool carries a hand-written implementation, pinned against the RFC 1321 test vectors
      and cross-checked against Node&rsquo;s own MD5 at every input length from 0 to 200 bytes.
    </p>

    <h2>Wordlists</h2>
    <p>
      The passphrase generator ships three lists: the BIP39 English list (2,048 words), the EFF
      short diceware list (1,296 words), and a 101-word superhero list. BIP39 and the EFF list are
      public domain; the superhero list is our own. All three are bundled with the page rather than
      fetched at runtime, and each is loaded only when you tick it.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can draw from several lists at once. Words that appear in more than one are counted only
      once — the lists overlap heavily, with 464 words shared between BIP39 and the EFF short list
      alone. Counting a shared word twice would claim more bits than the pool really holds and
      would make that word twice as likely to be drawn as any other.
    </p>
    <p>
      A short list is not a weak list, it just needs more words. Every word from the superhero list
      is worth 6.66 bits against BIP39&rsquo;s 11, so it takes nearly twice as many of them to reach
      the same strength. The bit count under each result is the number to watch, not the word
      count.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored</h2>
    <p>
      Your chosen theme and the option settings on each tool are kept in <code>localStorage</code>,
      in your browser, so the page comes back the way you left it. Nothing you generate is ever
      stored, anywhere — not in <code>localStorage</code>, not in memory beyond the current page
      view, and not on any server.
    </p>

    <h2>Verifying that</h2>
    <p>
      Open DevTools, switch to the Network tab and use any tool on this site. You will see the
      requests that loaded the page, and nothing after them.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
